{
  "title": "CHAPTER 20: ECONOMIC PLANNING IN INDIA",
  "order": 19,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "2dnrabe",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO ECONOMIC PLANNING"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The First Five-Year Plan was based on the Harrod–Domar Model, prioritizing agriculture, power, and irrigation.",
        "The Second Five-Year Plan adopted the Mahalanobis Model to lay the foundation of heavy industries and public sector expansion.",
        "External conflicts and severe droughts led to a temporary pause in long-term planning, causing the 'Plan Holiday' (1966–1969).",
        "Market-oriented LPG (Liberalisation, Privatisation, Globalisation) reforms were launched during the Eighth Five-Year Plan.",
        "NITI Aayog replaced the Planning Commission in 2015 to transition from centralized planning to cooperative federalism."
      ],
      "id": "17uvdd2"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
